# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Limenzy CRM — environment configuration
#
# Copy to .env.local and fill in the values from your Supabase project
# (Dashboard → Project Settings → API Keys). Never commit .env.local.
#
# This application uses the CURRENT Supabase key model — publishable and
# secret keys. The legacy `anon` and `service_role` JWTs are not supported
# under any name.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_* values are inlined into the browser bundle at BUILD time, so
# after changing them you must restart `next dev` or rebuild.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Browser-safe (public) -------------------------------------------------
# These are shipped to the browser by design. The publishable key is safe to
# expose: it carries no privileges beyond what Row Level Security allows.

NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# Absolute origin used to build email verification and password-reset links,
# e.g. https://crm.example.com — no trailing slash.
# Optional: when unset the request's own origin is used, which is correct for
# local development and preview deployments.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=

# --- Local development -----------------------------------------------------
# Run the local stack with `npm run db:start`, then copy ITS printed values
# into .env.local (git-ignored). See docs/development.md.
#
# The local stack prints a publishable key (sb_publishable_...) and a secret
# key (sb_secret_...). Only the publishable key goes in a NEXT_PUBLIC_
# variable. Local keys are generated by the CLI, are the same for everyone
# running this project, and grant nothing outside your own machine — but they
# still do not belong in this committed file.

# --- Server-only: the application's database connection ---------------------
# The runtime role `limenzy_app` — non-owner, NOBYPASSRLS, so row-level
# security applies to it in full. It is NOT a superuser and must never be one.
#
# Provisioned locally, never by hand:
#
#     npm run db:start && npm run db:reset && npm run db:role:local
#
# That writes this value into .env.local with a locally generated password.
# Server-only: it must never be given a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix, and runtime code
# must never fall back to DRIZZLE_TOOLING_DATABASE_URL, which bypasses RLS.

DATABASE_URL=

# --- Server-only: transport security for that connection --------------------
# PEM text of the certificate authority that signs the DATABASE SERVER's
# certificate. Server-only: it must never be given a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix.
#
# This is a PUBLIC certificate. It is not a password, not a private key, and
# not a secret — but it is still configuration, so it belongs in your
# deployment platform's environment settings rather than in this file or the
# repository. Never paste a `-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----` block here; the
# application refuses one.
#
# REQUIRED for every host except the local loopback literals (localhost,
# 127.0.0.1, ::1). Without it, a remote connection is refused rather than
# downgraded: the application will not connect to a hosted database over
# unverified TLS. With it, the certificate chain AND the hostname are both
# verified against this authority.
#
# Where to get it: for Supabase, Dashboard -> Project Settings -> Database ->
# SSL Configuration -> Download certificate. Paste the file's whole contents,
# including the BEGIN/END lines. A bundle of several CA certificates is fine.
#
# Setting it in a deployment platform: most accept multiline values directly.
# Where one does not, `$'...\n...'` or the platform's file-based secret
# mechanism both work — what matters is that the value arrives with its line
# breaks intact, because it is parsed as PEM, not repaired.
#
# DATABASE_CA_CERT=

# --- Server-only secrets ---------------------------------------------------
# This must never be given a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix and must never be imported
# from a Client Component. The secret key bypasses Row Level Security
# entirely and can administer every Auth user in the project; leaking it to
# the browser would expose every workspace's data.
#
# USED BY ONE MODULE, src/server/auth/member-emails.ts, for ONE operation:
# reading the email addresses of the members a workspace's Owner/Admin is
# already authorised to see (spec 159). auth.users carries row-level security
# with no policies, so no database role can read it; the addresses come from
# the Auth Admin API instead. See docs/architecture-decisions.md section 6.4.
#
# Optional. Without it the Users screen still works and says the Email column
# is not configured in this environment.
#
# It must come from the SAME project as NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL above. Never
# pair a local secret key with a hosted URL, or the reverse.
#
# SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=
#
# There is deliberately no MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL. Migrations run through the
# Supabase CLI, and the schema owner (`limenzy_owner`) is NOLOGIN — it owns the
# objects and never connects — so no migration credential exists to configure.

# --- Local tooling only ----------------------------------------------------
# Connection string used by local Drizzle Kit commands (introspection and
# candidate SQL generation). TOOLING ONLY:
#
#   * it is never the application's runtime connection;
#   * it points at the LOCAL database started by `npm run db:start`;
#   * genuine runtime/migration role separation is Milestone 1C-C and is not
#     implemented yet — this single local superuser URL is not that.
#
# DRIZZLE_TOOLING_DATABASE_URL=

# --- Outbound application email (optional) ---------------------------------
# Sending invitations by email. SEPARATE from Supabase Auth's own messages:
# confirmation and password-reset emails are sent by the Auth service with its
# own mailer, and nothing here affects them.
#
# Entirely optional. With these unset the product still works — an invitation
# is created and the link is copied by hand — and the Settings screen says so
# rather than offering to send something it cannot.
#
# Plain SMTP, so no provider is assumed. Any provider offers it, which keeps
# the production choice a matter of configuration rather than code.
#
# LOCAL DEVELOPMENT: the local stack already runs a test mailbox. Point these
# at it and invitations land in the Mailpit inbox at http://127.0.0.1:54324
# alongside the Auth emails. Nothing leaves the machine and no account is
# needed:
#
#   SMTP_HOST=127.0.0.1
#   SMTP_PORT=54325
#   EMAIL_FROM=Limenzy CRM <no-reply@localhost>
#
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=
# Optional: a local test mailbox needs no credentials. A real provider will.
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASSWORD=
# Set to "true" only for implicit TLS on port 465. STARTTLS on 587 negotiates
# itself and does not need this.
SMTP_SECURE=
# The From address. Must be one the sending account is allowed to send as, on
# a domain with SPF and DKIM set up — without those, invitations are filed as
# spam or refused outright.
EMAIL_FROM=
